.Dd September 23, 2026
.Dt PAZARI-AGENT.TOML 5
.Os pazari
.Sh NAME
.Nm pazari-agent.toml
.Nd configuration file for the pazari guest memory agent
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr pazari-agent 1
reads one TOML file, by default
.Pa /etc/pazari/agent.toml .
.Pp
.Nm pazari-agent Cm example-config
prints an annotated file carrying every key at its default.
A copy is installed with the package as
.Pa share/doc/pazari/examples/agent.toml .
.Ss Values
A
.Ar size
is a byte count, written either as a bare integer or as a number with a unit.
The binary units
.Cm KiB , MiB , GiB
and
.Cm TiB
count 1024 and the decimal units
.Cm kB , MB , GB
and
.Cm TB
count 1000, so
.Ql 64MiB
and
.Ql 64MB
are different numbers.
Units are case-insensitive and a space before them is allowed.
.Pp
A
.Ar duration
is a string, never a bare number, written as a number and a unit, or several of
each:
.Ql 500ms ,
.Ql 5s ,
.Ql 1min ,
.Ql 2s 500ms .
The units run from
.Cm ns
to
.Cm y .
Unless a key says otherwise a duration must be greater than zero and at most
.Cm 24h .
.Sh THE BRAKE
The settings in the
.Sx engine
table are three numbers and a set of timings.
The three numbers place the cgroup limits the agent writes, against
.Va P ,
the memory the guest currently has:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
memory.max  = P - service_reserve
memory.high = memory.max - burst_reserve      (at rest)
memory.high = memory.max - open_guard         (waiting on a grant)
memory.high = memory.max                      (out of room)
.Ed
.Pp
At rest the workload is braked a whole
.Cm burst_reserve
below the point where the kernel would kill it, and the distance between the
two is the room it has to grow into before the agent has to do anything.
When the kernel throttles the workload at
.Va memory.high ,
the agent asks the host for more memory and opens the band to
.Cm open_guard
below
.Va memory.max ,
so the workload may burst while the request is in flight instead of stalling
for the whole round trip.
The guard is what stops that burst from arriving at
.Va memory.max
before the grant does.
.Pp
How to size the three is covered under
.Sx SIZING ,
with the measured numbers behind the defaults.
.Sh SETTINGS
.Ss General
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm tick_interval No = Ar duration
How often the agent reads the workload cgroup and
.Pa /proc/meminfo .
The default is
.Cm 1s .
.Pp
This is the resolution of everything the agent decides: idleness is counted in
ticks, titration steps once a tick, and a throttle that the cgroup's own event
counters do not raise as a wake-up is not noticed until the next one.
It is also the floor under how quickly the agent can react to a grant.
Under a
.Cm 500ms
tick the round trip from asking the host for memory to seeing
.Va MemTotal
rise measures around
.Cm 300ms ,
and under a
.Cm 100ms
tick around
.Cm 120ms ;
see
.Sx SIZING .
A shorter tick costs two file reads and a
.Xr read 2
of
.Pa /proc/meminfo
each time.
.El
.Ss engine
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm service_reserve No = Ar size
Memory withheld for everything in the guest that is not the workload: the
agent, the init system, the logging stack, whatever else the guest runs.
Required; there is no default, and zero is accepted.
.Pp
It is subtracted from the guest's memory to give
.Va memory.max ,
so it is not a guarantee that those services get the memory, only that the
workload's hard limit is set below it.
Pair it with
.Va MemoryMin
on
.Va system.slice
if the guest's own services must be protected from reclaim as well.
Setting it too low lets a braked workload squeeze the agent that is braking it;
setting it far too high wastes every byte the host grants.
.It Cm burst_reserve No = Ar size
The standing headroom between
.Va memory.high
and
.Va memory.max
while the workload is progressing.
Required; must be greater than zero, and greater than
.Cm open_guard .
.Pp
This is the workload's runway.
A workload that grows faster than one grow round trip can be served needs a
burst reserve larger than the memory it can acquire in that round trip, or it
will spend its life throttled.
.It Cm open_guard No = Ar size
The sliver of
.Cm burst_reserve
the brake holds back while a grant is in flight.
Required; must be greater than zero and strictly less than
.Cm burst_reserve ,
a relation the agent checks at startup rather than when the file is parsed.
.Pp
With no guard at all, a workload that wants memory faster than the host can
supply it walks straight into
.Va memory.max
and is killed there, in under five seconds both times it was measured.
With a guard in place the kernel throttles the workload as it climbs through
the guard, and that climb is what the grant has to arrive within.
.Pp
Useful sizes do not scale with the reserve; see
.Sx SIZING
for where they begin and why.
.It Cm grow_step No = Ar size
How much more memory one grant request asks for, over what the guest already
has.
The default is
.Cm 512MiB .
.Pp
The agent asks for the larger of this and what the workload is already short
by, then rounds the total up to the guest's memory block size
.Pq Cm 128MiB No on x86-64 .
A step smaller than the block size therefore does not make the steps smaller;
it only makes them one block each.
.It Cm deny_backoff No = Ar duration
How long to wait before asking again after the host has refused.
The default is
.Cm 5s .
.Pp
The host may name its own
.Va retry_after_ms
in the refusal; this is what the agent uses when it does not.
.It Cm settle_grace No = Ar duration
How long a resize is left to settle before the agent stops waiting for it and
carries on.
The default is
.Cm 10s .
.Pp
The agent leaves this state as soon as
.Va MemTotal
reaches the target, so the grace is a timeout and not a delay.
It matters when a resize lands short: the guest's
.Va MemTotal
counts what the kernel made usable, which is less than what the device was
asked for.
.It Cm idle_threshold No = Ar fraction
The share of its budget the workload must stay under to count as idle.
Greater than zero and at most
.Cm 0.9 ;
the default is
.Cm 0.5 .
.It Cm idle_intervals No = Ar count
How many consecutive idle ticks it takes before the agent offers memory back.
Must be greater than zero; the default is
.Cm 3 .
.Pp
Together these two set the deadband between growing and shrinking.
.Cm idle_threshold
says how empty the cgroup has to look;
.Cm idle_intervals
multiplied by
.Cm tick_interval
says for how long.
Shorten the tick and that second half shortens with it, which is usually not
what is wanted: a workload between two bursts looks idle for a moment.
.It Cm shrink_step No = Ar size
How much one offer to release hands back.
Must be greater than zero; the default is
.Cm 256MiB .
.It Cm shrink_cooldown No = Ar duration
How long after a release before another one is allowed.
Zero is legal here; the default is
.Cm 30s .
.Pp
Releasing costs the workload something.
The agent reclaims the memory out of its cgroup before offering it, and the
workload pays again for every page it has to fault back in.
A short cooldown over a workload with a sawtooth working set gives the memory
back just in time to ask for it again.
.It Cm reclaim_swappiness No = Ar count
The
.Va swappiness=
argument appended to the
.Va memory.reclaim
writes that free memory before it is offered back.
From
.Cm 0
to
.Cm 200 .
Leave the key out to reclaim at the guest's own
.Va vm.swappiness .
.Pp
.Cm 0
keeps a release from pushing the workload's anonymous pages into swap, so what
is handed back is memory the workload had genuinely finished with.
It also means a guest with no free page cache has nothing to release.
.It Cm titrate_step No = Ar size
How far one titration step moves
.Va memory.high
while the agent is still finding the workload's working set.
Must be greater than zero; the default is
.Cm 64MiB .
.It Cm titrate_interval No = Ar duration
How long each titration step is given before the next one.
The default is
.Cm 10s .
.It Cm titrate_psi_threshold No = Ar percent
The workload's own memory pressure, in percent of wall time, above which
titration stops lowering the brake.
Greater than zero and at most
.Cm 100 ;
the default is
.Cm 5.0 .
.Pp
Titration runs once, when the agent starts over a workload whose demand it has
not yet seen.
It begins with the brake off, at
.Va memory.max ,
and lowers
.Va memory.high
by
.Cm titrate_step
every
.Cm titrate_interval
for as long as the workload is quiet, which means neither throttled nor over
the threshold.
It hands over to the steady band once the brake has come down to where steady
would put it.
.Pp
A busy workload holds titration where it is rather than pushing it back, so the
brake comes down to meet the working set instead of cutting into it.
A step far larger than the working set puts the brake in its steady place in
one move and learns nothing on the way; a step far smaller takes a long time to
get there.
.El
.Ss engine.exhaustion
What the agent does once the workload wants more than the ceiling allows.
The table is optional and defaults to
.Cm stall
with a
.Cm 60s
timeout.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqstall\(dq
Leave the band open at
.Cm open_guard
and let the workload degrade.
The workload is throttled, not killed, and keeps whatever progress it can make.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm timeout No = Ar duration
How long the workload may want more than the ceiling allows before the agent
stops holding the band open.
The default is
.Cm 60s .
.Pp
When it passes, the band collapses onto
.Va memory.max
and the workload is killed by the kernel at the first allocation it cannot
satisfy.
A stall is therefore a delay, not a posture: it buys the time for an operator
or an autoscaler to raise the ceiling, and if nothing does, the outcome is the
same as
.Cm kill .
Set a long timeout where a stalled workload is more useful than a dead one, and
a short one where a workload that cannot progress should get out of the way.
.El
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqkill\(dq
Let the workload reach
.Va memory.max
and be killed as a unit.
The agent arms
.Va memory.oom.group
so the whole cgroup dies together rather than losing one process and carrying
on half-alive.
Takes no other key.
.El
.Ss runtime
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm cgroup No = Ar path
The managed workload cgroup, as an absolute path under the unified hierarchy,
for example
.Pa /sys/fs/cgroup/workload.slice .
Required.
.Pp
Everything the agent negotiates for is what this cgroup accounts.
Memory charged anywhere else in the guest is memory the agent cannot see, and
so cannot ask for.
Getting a containerised workload into it is not always a matter of naming the
slice;
.Xr pazari 7
says where it goes wrong.
.It Cm boot_mem No = Ar size
What the guest booted with, excluding anything hot-plugged.
Required; must be greater than zero.
.Pp
This is what the agent tells the host it started from, and the floor it will
never offer memory back past.
.It Cm hotplug_debounce No = Ar duration
How long the hot-plug listener waits for one resize's uevent burst to end
before it re-reads
.Pa /proc/meminfo .
The default is
.Cm 250ms .
.Pp
One virtio-mem resize raises a uevent per memory block, so a debounce shorter
than the burst makes the agent read
.Pa /proc/meminfo
once per block for no gain.
.It Cm reclaim_chunk No = Ar size
How much one
.Va memory.reclaim
write asks the kernel for.
Must be greater than zero; the default is
.Cm 64MiB .
.Pp
A release is split into writes of at most this size, and the agent stops at the
first one the kernel will not satisfy.
Larger chunks reclaim faster and hold the workload up for longer at a time.
.It Cm thp_always_fatal No = Ar boolean
Whether
.Pa /sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage/enabled
set to
.Cm always
fails preflight rather than warning.
The default is
.Cm true .
.Pp
A workload on
.Cm always
faults its anonymous memory in 2MiB at a time, so a single fault can carry the
workload 2MiB past
.Va memory.high
instead of one page past it, and a guard sized for 4KiB overshoot is no longer
a guard.
Boot the guest with
.Va transparent_hugepage=madvise
instead.
Turn this off only where the guest cannot be rebooted and a degraded brake is
better than none.
.El
.Ss runtime.backend
Which adapter writes
.Va memory.high
and
.Va memory.max .
The table is optional and defaults to
.Cm cgroupfs .
Everything else the agent does to the cgroup, including
.Va memory.oom.group
and
.Va memory.reclaim ,
goes through
.Cm runtime.cgroup
either way.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqcgroupfs\(dq
Write straight to the cgroup's own files.
Takes no other key.
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqsystemd\(dq
Set the limits as runtime properties of a unit over the system bus, so that
systemd keeps them across a daemon-reload rather than overwriting them with
what the unit file says.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm unit No = Ar name
The unit carrying the limits as
.Va MemoryHigh
and
.Va MemoryMax ,
for example
.Ql workload.slice .
Required.
.El
.El
.Ss metrics
Leave the table out and the agent binds no listener and keeps no counters.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm listen No = Ar address
The address the Prometheus endpoint answers scrapes on, for example
.Ql 127.0.0.1:9651 .
Required when the table is present.
.Pp
Any
.Cm GET ,
on any path, is answered with the OpenMetrics text exposition; any other method
is answered with
.Cm 405 .
See
.Sx METRICS .
.El
.Ss transport
How the agent reaches
.Xr pazarid 1 .
Required.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqvsock\(dq
.Dv AF_VSOCK .
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm cid No = Ar id
The host's context id.
The default is
.Cm 2 ,
which is
.Dv VMADDR_CID_HOST .
.It Cm port No = Ar port
The port the daemon listens on.
The default is
.Cm 4650 .
.El
.It Cm kind No = Cm \(dqunix\(dq
An
.Dv AF_UNIX
socket, for a guest that is not a VM.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm path No = Ar path
The socket the daemon listens on.
Required.
.El
.El
.Pp
Both variants also take:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm handshake_timeout No = Ar duration
How long to wait for the host's
.Cm welcome
before giving up on the connection.
The default is
.Cm 10s .
.It Cm reconnect_min No = Ar duration
The first reconnect delay.
The default is
.Cm 500ms .
.It Cm reconnect_max No = Ar duration
The cap the reconnect delay doubles towards.
Must not be less than
.Cm reconnect_min ;
the default is
.Cm 30s .
.Pp
Reconnecting is the only recovery mechanism the protocol has, and it is always
sufficient: neither side keeps state across a connection.
A long cap leaves a guest braked at its last known limits for that long after
the host comes back.
.El
.Sh SIZING
The numbers below come from the pazari test harness, whose
.Cm measure-brake-crawl
and
.Cm measure-hotplug-rtt
scenarios take them in a guest with no swap, against a workload that faults
anonymous memory as fast as the kernel will let it.
They are the numbers the defaults were chosen from.
They are not constants: take them again on hardware that matters.
.Ss What the guard buys
A guest with 1152MiB of budget and a 512MiB burst reserve was put under a
workload that faults memory as fast as the kernel will let it, and then refused
any more memory.
What happened next was decided by the band the refusal left open.
The run was made twice, and both are given, because one of the arms is not
stable:
.Pp
.Bl -tag -width "open_guard = 256MiB" -offset indent -compact
.It no guard
.Va memory.max
in 4.7s and 4.9s.
.It Cm open_guard No = Cm 16MiB
.Va memory.max
in 35s and in 11s.
.It Cm open_guard No = Cm 64MiB
still alive at 360s, 18.2MiB and 17.2MiB into the guard.
.It Cm open_guard No = Cm 256MiB
still alive at 360s, 14.1MiB and 14.8MiB into the guard.
.El
.Pp
The guard does not buy time in proportion to its size, because the kernel's
throttle does not throttle in proportion to the overshoot.
The penalty it imposes at
.Va memory.high
grows with the square of how far past it the workload is, and saturates at two
seconds per allocation, so a workload that is far enough past
.Va memory.high
stops making progress altogether.
Both of the larger guards had flattened out between 14 and 18MiB of overshoot,
in both runs, with the whole guard still ahead of them.
.Pp
The useful sizes therefore begin at a threshold, which on this host was not far
above 16MiB.
Below it, a guard holds only for as long as the workload takes to cross it, and
that time varies: the 16MiB guard measured 35s in one run and 11s in the next.
Above it, a guard holds for as long as the workload is willing to sit there,
and the 64MiB and 256MiB guards measured the same in both runs.
The
.Cm 64MiB
default is the smaller of those two, with room left for a workload whose pages
are cheaper to fault than these.
.Pp
Two things lower that threshold.
A guest on
.Va transparent_hugepage=always
faults 2MiB at a time, so it crosses 512 times as much guard per fault; and a
guest with swap can page out under the throttle and keep making progress where
this one could not.
Measure again after changing either.
.Ss What a grow costs
A grow round trip is the time from the agent asking the host for memory to the
guest's
.Va MemTotal
rising.
Over 11 consecutive grows, against a host with memory to spare, it measured a
median of
.Cm 302ms
and a 95th percentile of
.Cm 402ms
with the agent ticking at
.Cm 500ms ,
and a median of
.Cm 118ms
against a 95th percentile of
.Cm 180ms
at a
.Cm 100ms
tick.
.Pp
Nearly all of that is the resize.
The leg from the agent's request to the host's answer came back inside the same
56ms scrape in most cycles, which is as fine as the measurement gets; the leg
from the answer to
.Va MemTotal
rising measured
.Cm 290ms
and
.Cm 118ms
in the two runs.
.Pp
Size
.Cm burst_reserve
against the round trip measured on the host it will run on, not against this
one.
A workload that faults memory in at a gigabyte a second will take 300MiB out of
its reserve while a round trip of this length runs.
.Ss What the brake can see
The agent reads
.Va memory.current .
It never reads
.Va Committed_AS ,
and neither should anything sizing the reserves.
.Pp
The same guest was run twice against the same plan, once with a one-gigabyte
arena mapped normally and once with it mapped
.Dv MAP_NORESERVE .
The committed arena charged
.Va Committed_AS
1020MiB at
.Xr mmap 2
time and held it there while the workload touched nothing and then touched
768MiB of it; the
.Dv MAP_NORESERVE
arena charged nothing at any point.
.Va memory.current
in the two guests agreed to within a megabyte throughout.
.Pp
Commit is charged to the mapping and usage to the touching, and only usage is
what a guest is holding.
Commit does decide whether a mapping can be made at all: the same test could
not map an eight-gigabyte arena without
.Dv MAP_NORESERVE ,
in a guest with about 1.6GiB of
.Va MemTotal
and no swap.
.Ss Choosing the three
Take them in that order, largest first.
.Pp
.Cm service_reserve
is the guest's own footprint with the workload stopped, plus room for it to
grow: read
.Va memory.current
of
.Va system.slice
on a running guest and add a margin.
.Pp
.Cm burst_reserve
is how much memory the workload can acquire in one grow round trip.
Multiply the round trip by the rate at which the workload faults memory in when
it is not throttled, and round up.
A reserve below that leaves the workload throttled between every grant; far
above it wastes memory that is granted and never used.
.Pp
.Cm open_guard
is how much of that reserve is kept back for the round trip itself.
Size it against the knee rather than as a fraction of anything: it has to be
past the point where the kernel's throttle saturates, which is the only thing
that makes a guard hold indefinitely rather than for a measured number of
seconds.
Find that point by lowering the guard until a refused workload starts reaching
.Va memory.max ,
and then leave several times the margin.
It still has to be small enough that the workload gets the rest of the reserve
to burst into, which is the other half of why it is the smallest of the three.
.Ss systemd-oomd
A braked workload sits at
.Va memory.high
and stalls there on purpose, which is indistinguishable from a workload in
trouble to anything watching pressure.
Keep
.Xr systemd-oomd 8
off the cgroup the agent manages:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ManagedOOMMemoryPressure=auto
ManagedOOMSwap=auto
ManagedOOMPreference=omit
.Ed
.Pp
.Va ManagedOOMPreference=omit
is what excludes the cgroup.
The other two only decline to opt in, and are there in case a parent slice
opted in for it.
.Sh STARTUP CHECKS
The agent runs the checks of
.Nm pazari-agent Cm preflight
before it starts, and refuses to run if any of them fails.
A warning does not stop it.
.Pp
Three further checks are made once the guest's memory geometry has been read,
and each is fatal:
.Va MemTotal
must not be below
.Cm runtime.boot_mem ;
it must cover
.Cm service_reserve
plus
.Cm burst_reserve
plus one memory block for the workload; and
.Cm open_guard
must be strictly smaller than
.Cm burst_reserve .
.Pp
They are checks on the geometry rather than on the file, so a configuration
that parses can still be one the agent refuses to start on.
.Sh METRICS
With a
.Sx metrics
table present, the agent publishes, under the prefix
.Va pazari_agent :
.Pp
Gauges:
.Va memory_high_bytes
and
.Va memory_max_bytes ,
the limits as last written;
.Va plugged_bytes ,
the guest's
.Va MemTotal ;
.Va ceiling_bytes ,
the ceiling the host named, or zero before it has;
.Va band_open ,
1 while the brake is off;
.Va workload_usage_bytes
and
.Va swap_used_bytes ;
.Va high_events
and
.Va max_events ,
the cgroup's own
.Va memory.events
counters;
.Va psi_some10 ,
the workload's memory pressure;
.Va connected ;
and
.Va mode ,
which carries a
.Va mode
label and is 1 on exactly one of
.Cm titrating , steady , awaiting_grant , releasing
and
.Cm exhausted .
.Pp
Counters:
.Va throttle_wakes_total ,
.Va grant_requests_total ,
.Va grants_total ,
.Va denies_total
with a
.Va reason
label,
.Va offers_total ,
.Va reclaims_total ,
.Va reclaim_asked_bytes_total ,
.Va exhaustions_total ,
.Va connections_total
and
.Va disconnections_total .
.Pp
The agent refreshes its gauges on every event it handles, not on a timer, so a
scrape sees the state as of the last thing that happened.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/pazari/agent.toml
The default configuration file.
.It Pa share/doc/pazari/examples/agent.toml
An annotated file carrying every key at its default.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
A guest that boots with 2GiB, keeps half a gigabyte for its own services, and
gives its workload a quarter-gigabyte of runway:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
tick_interval = "500ms"

[engine]
service_reserve = "512MiB"
burst_reserve = "256MiB"
open_guard = "64MiB"

[engine.exhaustion]
kind = "stall"
timeout = "300s"

[runtime]
cgroup = "/sys/fs/cgroup/workload.slice"
boot_mem = "2GiB"

[runtime.backend]
kind = "systemd"
unit = "workload.slice"

[metrics]
listen = "127.0.0.1:9651"

[transport]
kind = "vsock"
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pazari-agent 1 ,
.Xr pazarid 1 ,
.Xr pazarid.toml 5 ,
.Xr pazari 7
.Pp
.Xr systemd.resource-control 5 ,
